Task 6 — exact SearXNG sidecar identity receipt

Why:
- a numeric PID and command-line text cannot prove process identity after PID reuse;
- ambiguous state must block a second server instead of guessing or killing by name.

Implementation:
- strict bounded JSON receipt at the existing searxng-sidecar.pid authority;
- schema, PID, start time, native scope and full executable identity are validated; unknown, duplicate and zero fields fail closed;
- legacy numeric receipts are removed without signaling any PID;
- exact recovery compares every identity field then calls OwnedProcess::recover_exact;
- stale/reused identities remove the receipt, while invalid or ambiguous inspections retain it and block startup;
- identity is stabilized across consecutive OS observations and persisted immediately after spawn, before readiness;
- ps, PowerShell and process_text_matches were removed.

Controller review:
- found and fixed a swallowed Result that would have made ambiguous recovery fail open;
- moved receipt I/O off the async runtime thread;
- added exited-child reaping before ambiguity classification;
- real testing exposed an executable exec-transition after spawn, fixed by bounded identity stabilization before receipt publication;
- receipt cleanup remains after proven child termination/recovery.

Evidence:
- cargo test searxng::process_receipt --lib: 6/6;
- cargo test searxng::lifecycle --lib: 7/7;
- real owned Python process disappeared and its receipt was removed;
- invalid JSON remained on disk and blocked recovery;
- strict Clippy and fmt green;
- x86_64-pc-windows-gnu cross-check green (pre-existing CEF warnings only);
- graphify update and git diff --check green.

Limit:
- native Windows recovery execution is deferred to Task 7 release CI.
